---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import LeadForm from '../../components/LeadForm.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../config/site';
import { breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema } from '../../lib/schema';

const title = 'Mold & indoor air quality — when to test, when not to, and what actually fixes it | AuditYourHome';
const description =
  'Health Canada does not recommend routine air testing for mould — it recommends finding and fixing the moisture. When sampling is worth $300–$600, the 1 m² / 3 m² cleanup thresholds, and how the energy audit\'s moisture diagnostics find the cause sampling can\'t.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Should I pay for mold air sampling?',
    a: 'Usually not first. Health Canada explicitly does not recommend routine air testing for mould, because a spore count doesn\'t tell you where the moisture is coming from or what to fix — and any visible or smellable mould should be dealt with regardless of species. Sampling earns its $300–$600 when you suspect hidden mould inside walls or ceilings, when symptoms persist after visible mould is gone, or when you need documentation for a dispute or a clearance after remediation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is "black mold" the dangerous one?',
    a: 'Colour doesn\'t determine risk — many moulds look black, and Stachybotrys is only one of them. Health Canada\'s position is that any indoor mould growth should be removed and its moisture source fixed, whatever the species. That\'s also why speciation via sampling rarely changes what you actually do.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I clean it myself?',
    a: 'Small areas — up to about three patches totalling under 1 m² — yes: water and dish detergent (bleach isn\'t recommended), gloves, goggles, and an N95. Between 1 and 3 m², get a professional assessment first. A single patch bigger than 3 m², or contamination from sewage or a flood, is professional remediation territory. Porous materials like drywall that have mould through them get cut out, not wiped.',
  },
  {
    q: 'The house smells musty but I can\'t find anything. Now what?',
    a: 'That\'s the case for diagnostics before sampling: an energy audit\'s thermal imaging finds the cold, damp wall sections and hidden condensation planes, and its ventilation assessment establishes whether the house can actually exhaust the moisture it makes. If those point at a concealed cavity, targeted sampling or an inspection camera confirms it — you\'ll open the wall knowing why.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Testing guide', path: '/tests/' },
    { name: 'Mold & indoor air quality', path: '/tests/mold-iaq/' },
  ]),
  faqSchema(faqs),
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <div class="container">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/tests/">Testing guide</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> <span>Mold &amp; indoor air quality</span></nav>
    <div class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Specialty test guide · verified {SITE.dataReviewDate}</p>
      <h1>Mold &amp; indoor air: test the cause, not just the air</h1>
      <p class="lede measure">
        Here's the counterintuitive truth from Health Canada: it does <em>not</em> recommend
        routinely testing your air for mould. Mould is a symptom. The disease is moisture —
        condensation on cold surfaces, ventilation that can't keep up, leaks. Sampling has its
        place, but the sequence that actually fixes musty houses runs
        diagnose&nbsp;→&nbsp;fix&nbsp;→&nbsp;clean — and the diagnosis is what a good energy
        audit does best.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="glance">
      <div class="g"><p class="k">Health Canada</p><p class="v">No routine air tests</p></div>
      <div class="g"><p class="k">DIY cleanup</p><p class="v">&lt; 1 m² patches</p></div>
      <div class="g"><p class="k">Pro remediation</p><p class="v">&gt; 3 m², or sewage/flood</p></div>
      <div class="g"><p class="k">Sampling, if needed</p><p class="v">$300–$600</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="measure prose">
      <h2>The sequence that works</h2>
    </div>

    <ol class="steps measure">
      <li>
        <h3>Find the moisture, not the mould</h3>
        <p>Every mould problem is a moisture problem wearing a costume. Condensation on cold
        corners and window frames, a bathroom fan venting into the attic, a grading or gutter
        leak, a chronically damp basement — the audit's thermal imaging and humidity assessment
        locate these without opening walls.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Decide: ventilation problem or cold-surface problem?</h3>
        <p>The two get confused constantly and the wrong guess costs thousands. Too much humidity
        for the air exchange → fix ventilation (fans that actually duct outside, an HRV). Normal
        humidity condensing on uninsulated surfaces → fix insulation. The audit's measurements
        make the call.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Size the visible mould honestly</h3>
        <p>Up to three patches under 1 m² total: clean it yourself — water and detergent, gloves,
        goggles, N95, and toss porous materials that are mouldy through. 1–3 m²: get a
        professional assessment. One patch over 3 m², or anything from sewage or flooding:
        professional remediation.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Sample only with a reason</h3>
        <p>Hidden-mould suspicion behind a wall, symptoms that persist after cleanup, or the need
        for documentation — those justify an IAQ professional's air or surface sampling. A number
        without a suspected source is $300–$600 spent describing the symptom.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Fix the cause, then verify</h3>
        <p>After the leak is sealed, the fan ducted out, or the wall insulated, watch humidity
        (a $20 hygrometer; winter target roughly 30–50%) and confirm the musty smell doesn't
        return. If remediation was professional, a clearance check confirms the job.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="card card-pad callout measure">
      <h3>Mold and your retrofit — never insulate over an active problem</h3>
      <p class="mb-0">
        Insulating or air-sealing over damp, mouldy assemblies locks the problem in and can make
        it invisible until it's structural. The <a href="/plan/">retrofit sequence</a> puts
        moisture diagnosis before envelope work for exactly this reason — and ventilation
        upgrades (an HRV in a tightened house) are how the fix holds. If you flagged condensation
        or musty air in the planner, this is why it routed you here.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <div class="measure">
      {faqs.map((f) => (
        <details class="faq"><summary>{f.q}</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>{f.a}</p></div></details>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="card card-pad srcs">
      <h3>Sources</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/publications/healthy-living/addressing-moisture-mould-your-home.html" rel="noopener">Health Canada — Addressing moisture and mould in your home (no-routine-testing position, 1 m²/3 m² thresholds, cleanup guidance) (verified {SITE.dataReviewDate})</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="related">
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/tests/screener/">Which specialty tests does my home need? →</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/tests/">All tests</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/issues/condensation-mould/">The condensation &amp; mould issue page</a>
    </div>

    <LeadForm
      heading="Start with the diagnosis"
      sub="A registered advisor's moisture and ventilation diagnostics find the cause. Tell us what you're seeing — condensation, musty rooms, ice dams — and we'll match you."
      context="guide-mold"
    />
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .crumbs { padding-top: 24px; font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--faint); }
  .crumbs a { color: var(--muted); }
  .glance { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 12px; margin: 4px 0 26px; }
  .glance .g { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 14px 16px; }
  .glance .k { font-size: 11.5px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--faint); margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .glance .v { font-family: var(--mono); font-weight: 600; font-size: 15px; color: var(--ink); margin: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 840px) { .glance { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
  .prose h2 { font-size: 23px; margin: 26px 0 10px; }
  .steps { counter-reset: s; list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 10px 0 6px; display: grid; gap: 12px; }
  .steps li { position: relative; padding: 14px 16px 14px 58px; background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); }
  .steps li::before { counter-increment: s; content: counter(s); position: absolute; left: 16px; top: 15px; width: 28px; height: 28px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--heat); color: #fff; font-family: var(--mono); font-weight: 600; display: grid; place-items: center; font-size: 14px; }
  .steps h3 { margin: 0 0 4px; font-size: 16.5px; }
  .steps p { margin: 0; font-size: 14.5px; color: var(--muted); }
  .callout { border-left: 4px solid var(--heat); margin: 26px 0; }
  .srcs { border-left: 4px solid var(--steel); margin: 30px 0 10px; }
  .srcs ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; display: grid; gap: 6px; font-size: 14px; }
  .related { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px; margin: 8px 0 26px; }
</style>
